    {
      "key": "8ff67193-8aa0-4851-a2eb-b839f9b601eb",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Write Reusable Functions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ff67193-8aa0-4851-a2eb-b839f9b601eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dea56aaf-fe11-4141-9695-a3b9f436fdab",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Working individually or with a partner, they should jot the steps down on a scrap piece of paper in the order they think makes the most sense. After ordering, students will give their set of steps a short, descriptive name and share how they decided on the order.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the slide and remind students that the steps are intentionally out of order. Instruct students to jot them down on a scrap piece of paper in the order that makes the most sense to them. Let them know that there isn’t one “right” sequence. After arranging their steps, they should give the process a short, descriptive name and share with a neighbor how they decided on the order. Then, ask a few students to share with the class. Emphasize how giving their set of steps a clear name makes it easier for others to understand what it does—this will connect directly to how programmers name and reuse functions\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students have different ideas about the first step, use that as part of the discussion. Highlight that algorithms can vary depending on context and user goals."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dea56aaf-fe11-4141-9695-a3b9f436fdab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91ad2132-9e19-4a1e-88be-ebe1bf492c84"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f929a9d-f606-4b0a-8496-a41b9e1804cf",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f929a9d-f606-4b0a-8496-a41b9e1804cf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91ad2132-9e19-4a1e-88be-ebe1bf492c84"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "afd547dc-0a69-4ab1-b3a7-9dd98d874fe9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "afd547dc-0a69-4ab1-b3a7-9dd98d874fe9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91ad2132-9e19-4a1e-88be-ebe1bf492c84"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f2dd4064-ee65-42f5-a6c2-f9ced786ac51",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Connect their activity to the new vocabulary. Explain that what they just did — naming and reusing a set of steps — is exactly what programmers call a function. When we reuse those steps to make complex processes easier, we’re practicing abstraction.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f2dd4064-ee65-42f5-a6c2-f9ced786ac51",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91ad2132-9e19-4a1e-88be-ebe1bf492c84"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f394e12-dd75-4f12-8689-0948a55070df",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In programming, we call a reusable set of steps a function. It lets us do something again and again without rewriting all the details. When we reuse steps to simplify a bigger task, that’s called abstraction — it helps us focus on what matters instead of every tiny detail.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Let students know they’ll see the official definitions of function and abstraction in Levels 2–5, where they’ll have a chance to apply and revisit them. This slide is just meant to connect the warm-up to the coding concepts they’re about to explore.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f394e12-dd75-4f12-8689-0948a55070df",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91ad2132-9e19-4a1e-88be-ebe1bf492c84"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3e1057ef-1581-45b3-bdb3-5074131a5bae",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3e1057ef-1581-45b3-bdb3-5074131a5bae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91ad2132-9e19-4a1e-88be-ebe1bf492c84"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f0b3c97-8106-4f6c-85a2-30b873e32e41",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f0b3c97-8106-4f6c-85a2-30b873e32e41",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91ad2132-9e19-4a1e-88be-ebe1bf492c84"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4d4ff0e3-e74b-458f-9e75-e80553e49943",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students rename functions with descriptive, meaningful names and reinforce their understanding by adding or refining comments in their code.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Exploring Functions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4d4ff0e3-e74b-458f-9e75-e80553e49943",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8444cb70-9be2-4650-adaf-cfd8a5ffe5bc",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Front-end developers create code that others can easily read and reuse. One way to make code clearer is through refactoring—cleaning up and renaming parts of the code to reduce duplication. Today, you’ll act as front-end developers by making your code more readable and useful to others.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8444cb70-9be2-4650-adaf-cfd8a5ffe5bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "54e872f0-4ed7-46e8-8eb7-0117894d1b86",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 5.\n- Level 1: Students rename functions descriptively and add helpful comments.\n- Level 2: Students read the vocabulary definition for function.\n- Level 3: Students add their own explanation or example of function to the vocab free response.\n- Level 2: Students read the vocabulary definition for abstraction.\n- Level 3: Students add their own explanation or example of abstraction to the vocab free response.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, listen for the reasoning behind their renaming choices. Ask guiding questions like: “Why did you choose that name?” or “Would someone new to this code understand what that function does?”\n",
        "progression_name": "What's in a Name?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "54e872f0-4ed7-46e8-8eb7-0117894d1b86",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8d2124b0-6e8c-4f48-99ce-1fd169a44e8e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8d2124b0-6e8c-4f48-99ce-1fd169a44e8e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6616c567-918f-4aa2-ac5a-8660bec4443e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why do descriptive names for functions matter?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that descriptive names make code easier to read, understand, and maintain—for both themselves and others. Help them connect this to abstraction, explaining that a good name lets programmers focus on what a function does instead of how it works.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, invite a few students to share one example of a renamed function and explain why they chose that name. Highlight the importance of clarity and collaboration—well-named functions help teams (and even AI tools) interpret code more accurately.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6616c567-918f-4aa2-ac5a-8660bec4443e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "98dc5f4c-1233-439d-83f2-677ea17a1691",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "98dc5f4c-1233-439d-83f2-677ea17a1691",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2b1c1866-a3db-49d9-8ed3-c5baec2919c0",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show examples of functions and point out the similarities:\n- Both perform the same action (returning \"Hello!\").\n- The difference is mainly in syntax — arrow functions use => instead of the word function.\n- We still give them clear names and use them to reuse code.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2b1c1866-a3db-49d9-8ed3-c5baec2919c0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b6bc17dd-7240-4360-9ec9-50bd00799985",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the next few levels, you’ll start seeing functions that use arrows instead of the word function. Don’t worry — they work exactly the same way. The arrow just makes the code shorter and easier to read.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you sense hesitation, have students point to the parts that match between the two styles (name, parameters, body). Reinforce that programmers use both versions, but arrow functions are often preferred for concise, modern code.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b6bc17dd-7240-4360-9ec9-50bd00799985",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "222f8232-3cd9-4f8a-9183-aa4fa855aa7f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students practice creating and reusing arrow functions while naming them descriptively and commenting their purpose.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 6 through 8..\n- Level 6:Students extract an inline event handler and rewrite it as a named arrow function.\n- Level 7: With AI support, students find repeated code and refactor it into a single, reusable function.\n- Level 8: Students choose a context and create as many well-named, functional arrow functions as possible within the time limit.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, prompt them to describe what their function does and why they chose that name. Ask: “What does your function’s name tell you about its purpose?” or “How could you reuse this function in a different context?”\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Creating Functions",
        "progression_name": "Creating Functions",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For Level 8, ensure students have at least 10 minutes to explore. Encourage them to use AI to brainstorm new function ideas or to test whether their function names clearly describe what the code does before implementing it.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 6–8 to formatively assess student understanding of the lesson objectives. Look for evidence that students can:\n- Write and call arrow functions correctly.\n- Refactor repeated code into reusable functions.\n- Use clear, descriptive names and appropriate comments.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "222f8232-3cd9-4f8a-9183-aa4fa855aa7f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "633fd14f-ae57-43f7-b3d5-d71172875be4",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "633fd14f-ae57-43f7-b3d5-d71172875be4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f63be9f9-9ec6-47b8-bc92-781f9678f579",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Developers often review each other’s code to make sure it’s readable and understandable. Your job is to leave short, kind, and specific comments that help your peers see how clearly their code communicates its purpose.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f63be9f9-9ec6-47b8-bc92-781f9678f579",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2daeecb2-3821-4efc-b2f0-12e0b337cf84",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** At the end of the Mini-Hackathon, have students rotate to view 2–3 peers’ projects. Provide physical sticky notes for feedback. Their comments should focus on clarity, naming, and alignment between the function’s name, comment, and behavior.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Remind students to use descriptive language in their feedback. If they’re unsure what to say, prompt them with: “Is the name of the function clear?”, “Can you tell what this code does without reading every line?”, “Does the comment match the code’s behavior?”\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, students could update one of their functions based on peer feedback, demonstrating responsiveness to review — a nice parallel to the QA process.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2daeecb2-3821-4efc-b2f0-12e0b337cf84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "674e0225-0b39-4abb-9e2f-93d36abe6803",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "674e0225-0b39-4abb-9e2f-93d36abe6803",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "12976600-6c7c-46df-acbe-5781fa6eb9fa",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does writing clear, well-named functions make it easier for other people—or AI—to test, debug, and reuse your code?*\n- *What did you notice when reviewing a peer’s code that made it easier (or harder) to understand?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Highlight that clear, descriptive function names support readability, reuse, and testing. Students should recognize that part of being a QA engineer or collaborator is ensuring that code is easy to understand for both humans and AI tools that analyze it.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, have students identify one specific feedback comment they gave or received that improved clarity. Reinforce that peer and AI feedback both play a role in ensuring code is maintainable and reliable.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "12976600-6c7c-46df-acbe-5781fa6eb9fa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d702af6-dcc8-42db-a505-321894080891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "213784da-0972-41cd-af0d-1bd61fe3fe9b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students discuss their responses to the Question of the Day with a partner. After a few minutes, ask for volunteers to share with the class. Encourage students to use examples from their code today — such as how they renamed a function, created a reusable arrow function, or refactored repeated code — to explain how they made their code cleaner and easier to reuse.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students give general answers (like “I used functions”), prompt them to be more specific by asking follow-up questions such as:\n- “What change made your code easier for someone else to understand?”\n- “How did naming or reusing a function help simplify your program?”\n- “How might AI tools benefit from code that’s clearly named and organized?”\n\nThis reinforces both human and AI readability while helping students articulate what clean and reusable really mean in practice.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "213784da-0972-41cd-af0d-1bd61fe3fe9b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4a39bedf-e0b9-4249-a2dc-a0a079a6e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2061c418-c526-4edd-b436-2549dd6e7b48",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2061c418-c526-4edd-b436-2549dd6e7b48",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4a39bedf-e0b9-4249-a2dc-a0a079a6e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a342702f-aedc-4ed9-a947-d38fcc2189fc",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 9 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a342702f-aedc-4ed9-a947-d38fcc2189fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4a39bedf-e0b9-4249-a2dc-a0a079a6e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "87a1e7b2-be86-4303-8c22-5b84bff83d57",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What functionality of YouTube does this flowchart represent? What users would want the condition (yellow diamond) to evaluate to “Yes”? “No”?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that apps make decisions based on user input or app data, similar to the flowchart. Examples include YouTube deciding which videos to suggest next or login screens checking if username and password match. This helps students recognize that apps are full of decision-making points and anchors abstract conditionals in contexts they know. It also helps them understand that user input can determine the user experience, for example turning on Autoplay might help some users and frustrate others.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "87a1e7b2-be86-4303-8c22-5b84bff83d57",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e7d07585-293c-4cf2-be18-3ab6ccdb5f1e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "87192e89-319c-4e89-a95e-d332e731b494",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Predict & Trace: Toggle Mode"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "87192e89-319c-4e89-a95e-d332e731b494",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b8ab719-d956-4c3e-8df2-bd967fb1fea9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Generate with AI: Conditionals"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b8ab719-d956-4c3e-8df2-bd967fb1fea9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7b7764b3-390b-4611-bbe2-8c7ec8ea1d3f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Write it Yourself: Conditionals"
      },
