    {
      "key": "d82ab178-fec2-443d-b890-a27b652c1db7",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Sketch Lab: Create a Conditional Flowchart"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d82ab178-fec2-443d-b890-a27b652c1db7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6cf9c60-c658-4706-b3f0-4dcb14e3853a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Turn Your Flowchart into Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6cf9c60-c658-4706-b3f0-4dcb14e3853a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "45fe0a85-b535-4885-bd85-0cfdfefcbbf7",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students practice modeling decisions as flowcharts and understanding how those decisions can be represented in their code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 6.\n- Level 1: Students use Sketch Lab to build their own flowchart.\n- Level 2: Students read the vocabulary definition for flowcharts.\n- Level 3: Students add their own explanation or example of flowcharts to the vocab free response.\n- Level 4: Students review the panel level to understand how conditionals look in JavaScript.\n- Level 5: Students read the vocabulary definition for conditional statements and booleans.\n- Level 6: Students add their own explanation or example of conditional statements to the vocab free response.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are including all key components including a start, process, decision, and outcome. Each flowchart must incorporate at least one variable and at least one function. Help students who are not sure of what to flowchart by asking prompting questions like: \"What decisions did you make to get to class today?\" or \"In what order did those steps occur?\"\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Crafting Conditionals with Flowcharts",
        "progression_name": "Creating Conditionals with Flowcharts"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "45fe0a85-b535-4885-bd85-0cfdfefcbbf7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3cd3e8f-de73-4c9e-8491-344dfbbdba7b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Conditional Statements"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3cd3e8f-de73-4c9e-8491-344dfbbdba7b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "35a4d41f-21b8-49a4-b695-8e54b8063837",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "35a4d41f-21b8-49a4-b695-8e54b8063837",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "70331374-1324-4961-9ba5-140763045b73",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students partner up to trace through the different paths of their partner’s flowchart.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Does your partner’s flowchart make sense? Are the decision points clear?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should be able to articulate how their partner’s flowchart represents a decision-making process and receive constructive feedback on the clarity and completeness of their own flowchart. This peer feedback reinforces the concepts of algorithmic thinking and visual logic.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define flowchart.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define conditional statement.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define Boolean.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "70331374-1324-4961-9ba5-140763045b73",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82c59004-91d8-44c8-98dd-b01e895888bd",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Translate flowchart decisions into JavaScript if/else statements and use AI as a coding partner.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students review the flowcharts they made in Level 1. They should draft JavaScript if/else statements on Level 7 to represent the conditionals in their flowchart, making sure to incorporate at least one variable and at least one function into their code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Guide students to compare the flowchart logic directly to the code they are writing. Encourage them to use the AI to debug by asking how to debug incorrect conditionals or errors (e.g., 'Why is this conditional set up incorrectly? Help me fix it.')\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Turn Flowcharts to Code",
        "progression_name": "Turning a Flowchart into Code",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that the AI is a tool for learning and debugging, not a replacement for understanding. Encourage students to explain their code and the AI's suggestions to each other to make sure they understand it.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82c59004-91d8-44c8-98dd-b01e895888bd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f8c6a7c8-2910-4cdf-84d3-126e3c286117",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f8c6a7c8-2910-4cdf-84d3-126e3c286117",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "64ff176c-f8e1-4fd9-bc3d-fa078403ccca",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Consider an if-else conditional you included in your code. Who benefits from the decision evaluating to “True”? Who benefits from it evaluating to “False”?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should reflect on who benefits from rules that are implemented. This can be the user themself having a better or worse outcome, or someone else affected by the decision point. For example, consider a decision point based on the business hours of a restaurant. If a customer arrives after a restaurant’s kitchen has closed and is not able to order food, it creates a worse outcome for the customer but a better outcome for the restaurant staff who can close on time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete the Level 8.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Students are interpreting a conditional statement and answering a question about it. As students read over the question, ask them to explain their reasoning.\n",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student responses to gauge whether they can clearly explain how to debug conditional statements.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "64ff176c-f8e1-4fd9-bc3d-fa078403ccca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41ea9e59-eb4d-41ab-965f-af297127e31e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 9 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41ea9e59-eb4d-41ab-965f-af297127e31e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "56e37027-246f-4d02-accd-569687dc455d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16129a2f-5835-47e5-871b-8e533963854a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to imagine they are shopping online for clothing and click the \"Add to Cart\" button. Prompt them to think about what information the website needs to process this request. Record their ideas on the board. Ask students to consider what would happen if one of the pieces of information (like size, color, or quantity) was missing when they clicked \"Add to Cart\". Guide them to understand that functions need specific information to work correctly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16129a2f-5835-47e5-871b-8e533963854a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a84b66f1-e827-441c-bc56-5bb92ec0ac28"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3838490b-e04b-45e4-bb2d-c681008f59e6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Students will extend their flowchart knowledge by introducing new shapes for inputs (arguments) passed into a function and visualize how parameters enter and move through a function.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What new symbol do you notice? What is its purpose?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Introduce the blue rounded box symbol in flowcharts as representing input (parameters) for a process (function). Students should be able tp identify the inputs (color, size, material, and quantity) the process (function) requires. The purpose of these parameters is to make the Add to Cart process more reusable; there can be a single function that accounts for a wide variety of different shirts and properties.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define parameter.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How do parameters make a single function able to handle different variations (like different shirt sizes or colors)?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should articulate that parameters allow a single function to be reused with different data, making the code more efficient and adaptable. Guide the discussion to highlight that instead of writing separate functions for 'add small shirt' and 'add large shirt,' one function can handle both by accepting a 'size' parameter.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Parameters"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3838490b-e04b-45e4-bb2d-c681008f59e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a713f47c-7038-4483-a7c1-8a8408d4678a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Students will complete Lesson 6, Levels 1 through 3.\n- Level 1: Analyze a flowchart in Sketch Lab to identified repeated code that might be refactored into one parameterized function.\n- Level 2: Write clear, beginner-friendly docs for your refactored function from Sketch Lab.\n- Level 3: Trace the user flow within an actual application.\n",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Writing Functions with Parameters",
        "progression_name": "Exploring Functions with Parameters"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a713f47c-7038-4483-a7c1-8a8408d4678a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b7aa162e-5d66-444c-81c9-4a84f84e98b5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 4. Explain that students will now create their own parameterized function. Provide examples of contexts they could use (e.g., rendering a card, setting a status, updating a score). Instruct them to document their function in Markdown and then use AI Suggest Mode to help draft the JavaScript code, emphasizing that AI will provide hints rather than full solutions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Provide support and guidance as students design their functions. Offer suggestions for different contexts and help them refine their documentation and code. Encourage them to experiment with different parameters and their effects.",
        "progression_name": "Writing Functions with Parameters"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b7aa162e-5d66-444c-81c9-4a84f84e98b5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ad2e3cf-09bb-4983-aa32-3352d53e84c6",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ad2e3cf-09bb-4983-aa32-3352d53e84c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81ac71b4-95c1-4454-adca-59c3b8bc96c4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "81ac71b4-95c1-4454-adca-59c3b8bc96c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6bcbf80d-a429-4823-ac2e-f6777e27784a",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Facilitate a brief discussion about working with parameters. Prompt students to share specific examples of how their functions that they created were made more reusable and flexible by adding one or more parameters. Then ask students to share their experiences using the AI tool for coding assistance. Encourage them to discuss both the benefits (e.g., faster code generation, debugging hints) and the drawbacks (e.g., potential for incorrect suggestions, difficulty understanding the generated code).\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Normalize the idea that AI is a tool and not always perfect. Emphasize the importance of critical thinking and verifying AI-generated output. Discuss how AI can be a powerful learning aid when used effectively.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6bcbf80d-a429-4823-ac2e-f6777e27784a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "87dc3407-cdbe-457c-8ef5-e1a5af2241dd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "87dc3407-cdbe-457c-8ef5-e1a5af2241dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9d51cb3a-dd12-4f4c-b8c0-a19fb0c5ee75"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3111a1cd-fb83-4ecc-a93f-ae3b41cd28f5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3111a1cd-fb83-4ecc-a93f-ae3b41cd28f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9d51cb3a-dd12-4f4c-b8c0-a19fb0c5ee75"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f68cbf3c-1a65-4f72-8b95-da33bddde89f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the Understanding Assistive Technology video of a user with visual needs navigating a web page.\n\nQuestion to consider with the video:\n- What made navigating the site easy or hard for this user?\n- What changes could developers make in the code to improve this experience?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect the idea that accessibility isn’t about adding effects—it’s about designing code that communicates dynamically with different users. This prepares them to use variables, parameters, and event listeners to build pages that respond to user needs. Encourage students to refer to what they noticed in the video—how the screen reader described or skipped elements, and how the user interacted through swipes, double-taps, or the rotor feature.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f68cbf3c-1a65-4f72-8b95-da33bddde89f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f818420a-1655-4915-9f2e-bd4ec8dcc299"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb5011bb-a5ea-4cd4-a614-16b33ebaa11c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Accessibility means making sure your site works for everyone—not just how it looks, but how it responds.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb5011bb-a5ea-4cd4-a614-16b33ebaa11c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f818420a-1655-4915-9f2e-bd4ec8dcc299"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "871ca166-b281-4239-9e07-8d7cd2702c7b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "871ca166-b281-4239-9e07-8d7cd2702c7b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f818420a-1655-4915-9f2e-bd4ec8dcc299"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88702a74-2c38-423d-9dae-b1b83cb2ca7e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize common accessibility challenges that affect users and understand why improving accessibility is part of enhancing overall user experience. This understanding sets up the role of UX Designers, who focus on solving these issues through human-centered design.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce common accessibility issues.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Accessibility",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, invite students to identify which of these issues they’ve seen or experienced themselves.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88702a74-2c38-423d-9dae-b1b83cb2ca7e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ef23166-d0c8-4ba5-aa98-b528ce0d764f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ef23166-d0c8-4ba5-aa98-b528ce0d764f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "19318ea3-c094-42b0-9260-3d281900cb62",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the video, we saw one user experience accessibility challenges when navigating a page. These are some of the most common accessibility barriers people encounter online — like low contrast, missing image descriptions, small text, or unclear layouts. Addressing these kinds of issues isn’t just about fixing problems — it’s about improving the experience for everyone.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "19318ea3-c094-42b0-9260-3d281900cb62",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e728d96f-373d-466a-91e2-4b8837a58cdd",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain how UX Designers make sure websites and apps are easy to use and accessible for everyone. They research how people interact with technology, spot problems that make it harder to use, and design ways to fix them. Sometimes they use AI tools to test for accessibility issues—like checking contrast or detecting missing image descriptions—but they still rely on human judgment to make the best decisions about layout, tone, and user needs.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e728d96f-373d-466a-91e2-4b8837a58cdd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4e3c518-14a5-4387-9a57-9121edc9a162",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you’ll think a little bit like a UX Designer as you plan and code small improvements that make web pages more responsive and inclusive. We'll connect accessibility concepts to the JavaScript and DOM manipulation techniques we'll be practicing.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4e3c518-14a5-4387-9a57-9121edc9a162",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1394b5dc-147b-4431-9785-b9405cfa5978",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Building More Dynamic Pages activity guide.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1394b5dc-147b-4431-9785-b9405cfa5978",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a26f7ed2-7133-4be9-872e-cb2c62f4396e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’re going to be doing the work of a UX designer during an accessibility pass. You’ll spot issues, connect them to user benefits, and identify which exact element or style in the DOM a developer could update to create a better experience for that user.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a26f7ed2-7133-4be9-872e-cb2c62f4396e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f620583a-5199-4040-9023-93241d363a26",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you do this, focus not only on what needs fixing but also on how code can make the change happen dynamically—through variables, event listeners, or other interactive elements that respond to user needs.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f620583a-5199-4040-9023-93241d363a26",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8409e2ba-b069-4de7-bdda-7849979a43e6",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Send students to Level 1 to complete the a T-chart in their activity guide. The three columns should include:\n- Concept: Accessibility principle (e.g., High Contrast)\n- User Benefit: What improvement this creates for the user (e.g., Readability for users with visual impairments or bright lighting)\n- DOM Hook: The part of the code or element that can be updated to achieve that change (e.g., Toggle a class like theme-dark or high-contrast)\n\nRemind students that the T-Chart connects human needs to the specific technical hooks developers use to meet those needs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, ask: “Which code element or variable might control that behavior?” or “How could this improvement be made responsive instead of static?” Encourage them to think about how code logic helps meet the user’s needs, not just what problems exist.\n",
        "progression_name": "Accessibility Concepts",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle to connect accessibility concepts to the DOM, prompt them to think about changeable attributes. For example:\n- Color contrast → class or style updates\n- Font size → variable or CSS property controlled by input\n- Layout clarity → toggling visibility or simplifying content through event listeners\n\nEncourage students to share one “dynamic helper” idea they think could make a big difference for users.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8409e2ba-b069-4de7-bdda-7849979a43e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "77f4eb2f-a320-4875-b6b5-d6f863593695",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "77f4eb2f-a320-4875-b6b5-d6f863593695",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed0b2dfe-e03c-4662-8e96-14b2ca15398e",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Even though AI tools can identify accessibility issues, it’s still up to UX designers and developers to decide which changes actually improve the user experience. Accessibility is more than fixing problems—it’s about designing features that respond to people’s needs. In today’s activity, you’ll redesign a webpage to make it more dynamic and inclusive for a specific user.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Accessibility Redesign",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed0b2dfe-e03c-4662-8e96-14b2ca15398e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6043dc29-ed33-43e1-8ef7-a4501f869dcf",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Levels 2 and 3.\n- Level 2: Students choose a starter website and user persona, then plan which accessibility improvements to make in their activity guide.\n- Level 3: Students review the accessibility feature menu to learn about possible changes and decide which ones best serve their user.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, ask: “What will your feature respond to—a click, an input, or another event?” and “How could this feature adapt dynamically as a user interacts with the page?” Remind students that dynamic features use variables and event handlers to respond to user actions, rather than static, one-time edits.\n",
        "progression_name": "Redesign for a User"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6043dc29-ed33-43e1-8ef7-a4501f869dcf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59564248-7699-4e0d-bf93-a06e7e18a232",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Menu of Accessibility Features",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to design for response and interaction. Prompt students to connect each improvement to a real user benefit, not just a visual change.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this activity to formatively assess how well students demonstrate the lesson objectives. Look for evidence that students can:\n- Use JavaScript to change styles, swap images, or show/hide elements in their project.\n- Apply variables and parameters to make those DOM changes dynamic and event-driven.\n- Revise interactions to improve accessibility and user experience, and explain how they balanced trade-offs between usability, design, and complexity.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59564248-7699-4e0d-bf93-a06e7e18a232",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44a9a997-6ac2-4faa-80e9-01ab8b2ac1ff",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44a9a997-6ac2-4faa-80e9-01ab8b2ac1ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff3700dd-3a36-4623-a42c-8398ab3ca84a",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Students rotate through the classroom (or share projects digitally) to demo their accessibility features. As they explore, they should identify:\n- The change: What was added or improved?\n- The behavior: Does it respond dynamically to user actions or settings (e.g., slider, toggle, user input)?\n- The impact: Who benefits and how does it improve accessibility?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students rotate, prompt with questions that push students to think beyond visuals and into the logic of interaction and inclusivity, such as: “How does this feature change based on what the user does?”, “Would this improvement help more than one kind of user?” and “What trade-offs might this feature create?”\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, wrap up with a quick share-out:\n- Which feature impressed you most and why?\n- Did you notice any trade-offs between usability and complexity?\n\nUse this as a bridge into the final discussion or reflection — it reinforces accessibility as both a design and coding challenge.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff3700dd-3a36-4623-a42c-8398ab3ca84a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0f789a6-34de-4c93-ac10-436f8fd0a63b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1f03cb6-8c4a-45b3-a860-37328c96db05",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What trade-offs did you have to make when improving your page’s accessibility or interactivity?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that real-world design often involves trade-offs between accessibility, aesthetics, and functionality. They reflect on how they balanced competing priorities to best serve their users—just like real UX designers do. Encourage students to share examples where they simplified, redesigned, or adjusted their features to improve accessibility (for example, reducing motion or increasing contrast). Use this discussion to reinforce the third lesson objective: Revising interactions to improve accessibility and balance trade-offs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 4 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1f03cb6-8c4a-45b3-a860-37328c96db05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "897cb38f-da38-4924-abfe-2a1f1c2ab8a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d2830d1e-0cff-4a18-b15b-39da37f7d909",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct students to follow the paths on the flowchart that apply to them and complete the corresponding task. Emphasize that they should be ready to explain the path they took.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Based on what a classmate is wearing, which path would they follow in the flowchart? What ending task would they complete?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students practice tracing nested decisions step-by-step, recognizing how each condition leads to a specific outcome. This builds understanding that nested conditionals evaluate one condition inside another to reach a final result.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d2830d1e-0cff-4a18-b15b-39da37f7d909",
        "lesson_activity.key": "456fe5bb-2199-4edb-9127-15232f34397b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ea16792b-1b3b-4a3e-8aed-c516e572a1ac",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build foundational understanding of nested if/else statements by translating a simple flowchart into working code. They’ll learn how each decision branch connects to a UI change, define what nested conditionals are, and use AI support to debug and confirm that all paths behave as expected.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Nested Conditionals activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 3, where they translate a provided flowchart into nest if/else statements with AI assistance.\n- Level 1: Students will label and map a provided flowchart into nested if/else statements.\n- Level 2: Students will read the definition nested conditional.\n- Level 3: Students will define nested conditional in their own words.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students implement their nested if/else logic, check that their code directly maps to the flowchart. Ask them to explain how each branch changes the UI and how they’re using test cases to verify correctness.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Flowchart to Code",
        "progression_name": "From Mini-Flowchart to Nested If/Else"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ea16792b-1b3b-4a3e-8aed-c516e572a1ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93780ca2-8bde-4701-b773-3d743289cec5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93780ca2-8bde-4701-b773-3d743289cec5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "91ba6dcb-9ed5-49d9-a4ad-d65f4da25712",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How does nesting conditions add depth to our logic?*\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that nesting creates a hierarchy of decisions, allowing for a more granular control flow that mirrors complex real-world scenarios and flowcharts.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "91ba6dcb-9ed5-49d9-a4ad-d65f4da25712",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a0e67753-6f1c-44c9-bf15-e9a27a5d87de",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students apply their understanding of nested if/else logic by planning, coding, and testing a multi-decision feature. They’ll connect their flowchart to working code, use AI to debug and refine logic, and verify that all four paths behave as intended through systematic testing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 4 and 5 by planning nested decisions and finally implement them in code. They will use scaffolds like the Test Cases table in their activity guide and AI support to refine their work.\n\n- Level 4: Students will plan a feature by creating a flowchart with nested decisions and test cases.\n- Level 5: Students will implement their plan in code, debug using test cases, and refine with AI support.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students implement their nested if/else logic, check that their code directly maps to the flowchart. Ask them to explain how each branch changes their web app and how they’re using test cases to verify correctness.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Plan, Code, and Test",
        "progression_name": "Plan in Sketch Lab"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a0e67753-6f1c-44c9-bf15-e9a27a5d87de",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "445b8443-6e63-4d44-95ee-72d1a1c432fd",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Code and Test"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "445b8443-6e63-4d44-95ee-72d1a1c432fd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5b9b8ce-ec7c-4b2b-b746-ae91dd88ac8b",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5b9b8ce-ec7c-4b2b-b746-ae91dd88ac8b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cc903e6-d289-4af0-b075-b43a7200f044",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What paths in your flowchart did AI miss?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students may notice that the AI assumed one condition mattered more (e.g., finished movie = true) — a small example of bias in logic or oversimplification.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Prompt students to connect this debugging step to real-world examples of biased or incomplete algorithms (e.g., recommendation systems showing only one kind of result).\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cc903e6-d289-4af0-b075-b43a7200f044",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41fcf392-9409-4249-9e75-a266fabca308",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete the sentences as an exit ticket:\n- 'One thing a nested if/else enables in my app is ___.'\n- In my flowchart, branch ___ maps to this exact code line: ___.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Have a pair project their flowchart and briefly trace how a specific input traverses branches to a UI result in their code. Bonus: 'A test case that broke then passed after debugging was ___ (why?).'\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41fcf392-9409-4249-9e75-a266fabca308",
        "lesson_activity.key": "141bc852-7894-47e3-be59-bbca6ebdf62a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1848f1fb-6e7a-4379-9df6-ddd07c55bd6a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the two example \"style rules\" on the slide. Have students come up with their own rule, using either 'and' or 'or' and discuss it with a partner. Emphasize that these should be school-appropriate, positive descriptors only.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1848f1fb-6e7a-4379-9df6-ddd07c55bd6a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "405289d2-7562-4879-a8a5-53d250e0a1bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1bc7fd2f-1679-4baf-b120-cf5cbc1e201d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we're going to explore how combining conditional statements using AND and OR can make our programs more robust. Real web apps often need to check more than one condition at a time. Simple if-else statements can only handle one test, but combining conditions helps your code make smarter decisions — like showing confidential information only if a user is logged in AND verified.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1bc7fd2f-1679-4baf-b120-cf5cbc1e201d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "405289d2-7562-4879-a8a5-53d250e0a1bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf044fd9-785a-4362-ad37-0c64d8f5e709",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf044fd9-785a-4362-ad37-0c64d8f5e709",
        "lesson_activity.key": "405289d2-7562-4879-a8a5-53d250e0a1bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6865378-3766-4944-824f-b28006df67ed",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will be able to explore web pages that use combined conditional statements with && (AND) and || (OR).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Combining Conditions with AND/OR activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 8. Explain that each checkbox sets a Boolean value (True or False). Have students work in pairs to predict the message outcome for each combination of checkboxes. Then have them toggle the checkboxes to test their predictions and record their observations in the activity guide.\n- Level 1: Test the AND operator to produce a message for all four outcomes: TT, TF, FT, FF.\n- Level 2: Test the OR operator to produce a message for all four outcomes: TT, TF, FT, FF.\n- Level 3: Students read the vocabulary definition for AND (&&).\n- Level 4: Students add their own explanation or example of AND (&&) to the vocab free response.\n- Level 5: Students read the vocabulary definition for OR (||).\n- Level 6: Students add their own explanation or example of OR (||) to the vocab free response.\n- Level 7: Students read the vocabulary definition for truth table.\n- Level 8: Students add their own explanation or example of truth table to the vocab free response.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Combining Conditions with AND/OR",
        "progression_name": "Using AND and OR"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6865378-3766-4944-824f-b28006df67ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0bef0684-b2ac-4f25-8282-6a85a53a0936",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0bef0684-b2ac-4f25-8282-6a85a53a0936",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "066edbf6-e925-44be-ad09-f305d125ffe7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How does changing the operator from && to || affect the outcome?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should be able to articulate how AND and OR operators change the logic of conditional statements. Guide the discussion towards understanding that AND requires all conditions to be met, while OR requires only one.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define AND (&&).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define OR (||).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define truth table.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "066edbf6-e925-44be-ad09-f305d125ffe7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "95bb0890-e89e-4ce6-b889-b6cbdd1954bc",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will create a flowchart to map out operator-based conditions. They will turn their flowchart into code in the following level.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Levels 9 and 10. Instruct students to choose a scenario and flowchart their rules in Sketch Lab. Students must ensure that there are outcomes for all four test cases (TT, TF, FT, FF) for their chosen context. Then, have them turn their flowcharts into code. They should swap in the appropriate AND (&&) or OR (||) operators and adjust any comparison operators (e.g., >= 13).\n- Level 9: Flowchart your context in Sketch Lab. Include at least two decision points and outcomes for all 4 test cases.\n- Level 10: Convert the Sketch Lab flowchart into code. Test out the code for all 4 possible outcomes.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Coding a Multi-Condition Flowchart ",
        "progression_name": "Creating a Multi-Decision Flowchart"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "95bb0890-e89e-4ce6-b889-b6cbdd1954bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5dc0d803-bd15-4026-a348-eb16566fcb03",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Code Your Rule",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider having students attempt the longest chained conditional (multiple && and || operators) they can create. Encourage them to feed it to AI Tutor to create edge cases for them to test.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5dc0d803-bd15-4026-a348-eb16566fcb03",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ba6b7e9-3eff-48b0-9d61-18ac20bda00c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 11 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ba6b7e9-3eff-48b0-9d61-18ac20bda00c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1df1a9ab-dab4-41e4-8499-f8e04a7b1ea0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1e341dda-5e82-4111-81e4-72714a880d12",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the NOT Simon-Says-style activity. Call out the idea that 'not false is true.' and 'not true is false'. Read random statements from the Opposite Simon Says Statements resource to facilitate the activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1e341dda-5e82-4111-81e4-72714a880d12",
        "lesson_activity.key": "676a8f12-7bda-4836-a98e-beddb2d0306b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e4ce8b3f-4492-4a1b-b8f5-29ff5183a730",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Show how a Boolean flag flips with ! and drives visual changes (image swap or show/hide), bridging to real products that use toggles.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Send students to Level 1. Have them explore, predict, observe, and try to name the pattern.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "The NOT Operator",
        "progression_name": "Toggle Thinking"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e4ce8b3f-4492-4a1b-b8f5-29ff5183a730",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ed21b23-e838-4a21-8441-8b0437b14cfb",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What did you notice? What did you wonder?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Elicit observations about the immediate visual feedback and the underlying state change. Encourage students to ask questions about how the code makes this happen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define NOT (!).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What are some examples outside of the classroom where a toggle or a 'not' condition is used?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Connect the concept of NOT and Boolean toggles to everyday technology. Organize examples across different categories to highlight the broad application. Examples: mute/unmute, password visibility, like/unlike, captions on/off, dark mode.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ed21b23-e838-4a21-8441-8b0437b14cfb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d68bccba-36c6-4e48-a2a9-45498afc7f2a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Students independently implement a UI toggle using the NOT operator, then extend the idea into a mini-project that combines Boolean logic with considerations of fairness and inclusivity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Adding NOT and Complex Conditions activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 4 through 6.\n- Level 4: Students build a simple toggle that flips a Boolean flag with ! and updates the UI (for example: like/unlike, password visibility, dark mode).\n- Level 5: Students build a mini-project feature using !, &&, and ||, testing it with a logic table.\n- Level 6: Students consider the fairness and inclusivity of their rule.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check whether students can explain how the NOT operator flips their flag and how that change updates the UI. In Level 6, prompt students to connect their logic rules to fairness and inclusivity, asking: “Whose experience improves with this rule? Could anyone be excluded unintentionally?” Students should use the Activity Guide to plan their feature.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Code a Toggle",
        "progression_name": "Code a Toggle with NOT"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d68bccba-36c6-4e48-a2a9-45498afc7f2a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7741d0bc-4511-44cd-a483-a9b1e74d7032",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Complex Rules with ! + && + ||"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7741d0bc-4511-44cd-a483-a9b1e74d7032",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "65dadcda-f5b4-45df-8029-ad0192c58f15",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to use the AI-assisted prompt to scaffold their code: “Given my IDs and goal, write the minimal handler that flips a Boolean and updates the UI. Explain how the NOT operator changes the value.” Remind them that the goal is to see how ! flips the state and connects directly to a UI update.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "65dadcda-f5b4-45df-8029-ad0192c58f15",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ba6cd3de-9269-4c57-bcac-d412e7b5ed41",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ba6cd3de-9269-4c57-bcac-d412e7b5ed41",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab2f5345-18ba-4b22-b32f-894db8333b7c",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the State Flip Pattern that students encountered today. Step one starts with creating an event listener. When the event occurs, the NOT operator can be used to switch the boolean logic of a variable from true to false, or vice versa. Finally, the content onscreen is rendered in order to display the change that occurs.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab2f5345-18ba-4b22-b32f-894db8333b7c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6c0103c-f88a-4dd1-a8dc-dfb8499512fb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 6 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6c0103c-f88a-4dd1-a8dc-dfb8499512fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "988d6b5c-9198-4a2e-99f7-224be3e8f64e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "837d709a-e5a4-4585-841a-5edcd5301983",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s an example of an app, game, or website that reacts in a way you really like ? Why do you think it feels satisfying or fun?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify qualities of engaging interactivity to inspire their own app designs and ground today’s planning in real user experiences.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "837d709a-e5a4-4585-841a-5edcd5301983",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "474b6a12-a5a5-46d2-a4a8-63866ed0f078",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Reactive Logic Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the first part of the project requirements.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to read the rubric on the last two pages of the project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice about the project requirements and rubric? What does success look like for this project?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students share what they notice about the project requirements and rubric and identify the components to implement in their program.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the milestone structure of the project. Explain that the Reactive Logic Project is divided into four milestones, each with its own goals and deliverables. Use the visual roadmap to preview the journey students will take — from defining their magazine concept to showcasing their final product.\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that milestones help them manage time and track progress, just like in real design and development projects. Encourage them to think of each milestone as a \"checkpoint\" to reflect on what's working, what needs improvement, and how their design aligns with their user and purpose.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "474b6a12-a5a5-46d2-a4a8-63866ed0f078",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5b3aeb8-7c92-45b6-bd93-f71624a705c4",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5b3aeb8-7c92-45b6-bd93-f71624a705c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "086dd250-cef1-42d0-8cd8-ed84ad41d1e4",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that today's focus is Milestone 1: Define Your Magazine, where students plan their magazine's concept, audience, and design direction. Direct students to open their Reactive Logic Project Guide and locate the Milestone 1 section. Have them read through the Goals and Deliverables so they understand what needs to be completed by the end of this milestone.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to highlight or note parts of the deliverables that feel most exciting or challenging. This helps you gauge engagement and plan support for different groups.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "086dd250-cef1-42d0-8cd8-ed84ad41d1e4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3426799d-767b-4ab3-a83c-5bed378c0231",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3426799d-767b-4ab3-a83c-5bed378c0231",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "33614ba6-634a-4b99-bb80-6063ba85dc2b",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct student to Level 1 to begin Milestone 1: Plan Your Interactive App. Students can choose which tool best supports their current task: brainstorming ideas, organizing their plan, or sketching out their logic and design.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students explore the hub, ask guiding questions such as:\n- “What type of interaction or outcome are you imagining for your app?”\n- “Which of these tools will help you move your idea forward right now?”\n- “How does your planned logic connect to the kind of experience you want your user to have?”\n\nUse these check-ins to confirm students are moving from idea to planning, rather than jumping straight into code.\n",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to experiment with multiple tools as they develop their plan — for example, brainstorming with the Idea Bot before mapping logic in the Flowchart Bot or sketching layouts in Sketch Lab. Remind them that testing and refining their ideas here will save time later once they start coding.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Set expectations for pacing — Milestone 1 can typically be finished by the end of today's class or early next class. Encourage students to focus first on clarity of idea rather than polish.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "33614ba6-634a-4b99-bb80-6063ba85dc2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f7976ad4-c9b4-43a3-a96b-e2e0ea87d1b4",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f7976ad4-c9b4-43a3-a96b-e2e0ea87d1b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "69b48c23-9a73-425d-beec-0eaedd6fa599",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 1 Reflection section in their project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are discussing and sharing these reflection questions with a thought partner.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few volunteers to share aloud or conduct a quick pair-and-share to close.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "69b48c23-9a73-425d-beec-0eaedd6fa599",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bafeeda5-d6ce-4722-8d28-1e2bfe595fe7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s one part of your app that you’re most excited to see working? What’s one piece you’re not sure how to build yet?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students articulate clear goals and challenges for their first build session, helping them prioritize tasks and seek targeted support.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bafeeda5-d6ce-4722-8d28-1e2bfe595fe7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa478d88-f0d4-45af-9e1b-785af3f759a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "01a86911-8e22-4e45-b50c-a78252320303",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 2 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on completing Milestone 1 and starting work on Milestone 2. Direct students to open their Reactive Logic Project Guide and locate the Milestone 1 and 2 sections. Have them review the Goals and Deliverables for each milestone so they understand what to prioritize during work time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1 and 2 to work on completing their planning and moving to drafting their app. Frame this transition as the point where their ideas become interactive. Encourage students to build one working piece before adding more complexity — professional developers also prototype small, testable pieces first. If students feel unsure where to start, remind them to revisit their flowchart and focus on building the first clear cause-and-effect moment from their logic plan.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task and progressing toward a complete first draft. Ask check-in questions like:\n- “Do your flowchart and wireframe clearly show what the user can do and what will happen?\n- “What’s the first part of your plan you’ll try to build today?”\n- “How will you know your first interaction is working correctly?”\n\nProvide quick support if students get stuck — redirect them to the AI Chat or Project Support Bot for troubleshooting help before intervening directly.\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "01a86911-8e22-4e45-b50c-a78252320303",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa478d88-f0d4-45af-9e1b-785af3f759a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6bae210-dd8a-4270-9772-2b520a2c6637",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 2 Reflection section in their project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write and discuss, listen for reflections that identify concrete next steps. Encourage students to document these next steps clearly so they can pick up with them in the next class period.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few students to share one key challenge or problem they plan to solve. Highlight that thoughtful reflection is a sign of growth—it shows they're thinking like real designers preparing to polish their final product.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6bae210-dd8a-4270-9772-2b520a2c6637",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa478d88-f0d4-45af-9e1b-785af3f759a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9d07fb5-3317-4ae1-b183-e8063de79896",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s one bug, glitch, or weird behavior your app is showing right now?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students practice describing and analyzing problems out loud, normalizing debugging as part of the creative process and preparing for peer or AI-assisted troubleshooting.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9d07fb5-3317-4ae1-b183-e8063de79896",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4579f2b8-8633-4cca-abac-4c0b109b5179",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 3 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on Milestones 2 and 3. Direct students to open their Reactive Logic Project Guide and locate the Milestone 2 and 3 sections. Have them review the Goals and Deliverables for each milestone so they understand what to prioritize during work time.\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out that these milestones move from building to refining. Encourage students not to aim for perfection at first—just to get their layout functioning, then improve it through feedback and iteration.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4579f2b8-8633-4cca-abac-4c0b109b5179",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "43f26543-d7d6-4660-a72c-b73ac5016774",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "43f26543-d7d6-4660-a72c-b73ac5016774",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c42355bb-a76a-4416-b3c2-fb1dd6dbd674",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the TAG Peer Review process and model what thoughtful feedback looks like. Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace or comment within the project guide if using shared docs. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it's a chance to gather early insights as part of Milestone 3.\n",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product.\nReinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance.\nIf time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c42355bb-a76a-4416-b3c2-fb1dd6dbd674",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5fd70c7e-9fde-48cf-b224-5eb671e1c062",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5fd70c7e-9fde-48cf-b224-5eb671e1c062",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16594286-5e31-43f1-983c-b7733b3ff92e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 3 Reflection section in their project planning guide. Let students know they can continue to add on to this reflection as they continue to make progress on Milestone 3 and conduct more testing.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, invite a few students to share how they’ve been testing their app or what they plan to test next. Emphasize that repeated testing and refinement mirrors what real software engineers do—continually running, observing, and adjusting their programs to make them stronger and more reliable. This helps students see debugging and iteration as essential parts of the professional development process, not just final clean-up.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16594286-5e31-43f1-983c-b7733b3ff92e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b3bba81-a8ca-427e-8e8b-5f639cd66b4a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If someone were testing your app right now, what’s the one thing you’d want them to notice first?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on the purpose and user experience of their app, setting an intentional focus for peer testing and feedback during today’s class.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b3bba81-a8ca-427e-8e8b-5f639cd66b4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bdbe1a-3cf7-4431-9576-e2bb7d5cd5d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4f3f7ce1-81b6-4b38-ac02-182b891c521b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 4 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on Milestones 3 and 4. Direct students to open their Reactive Logic Project Guide and locate the Milestone 3 and 4 sections. Have them review the Goals and Deliverables for each milestone so they understand what to prioritize during work time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the TAG Peer Review process and model what thoughtful feedback looks like. Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace or comment within the project guide if using shared docs. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it's a chance to gather early insights as part of Milestone 3.\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 4",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product.\nReinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance.\nIf time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4f3f7ce1-81b6-4b38-ac02-182b891c521b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bdbe1a-3cf7-4431-9576-e2bb7d5cd5d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e96f7f4-1e89-4dda-9eb7-ea2faeb0539b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e96f7f4-1e89-4dda-9eb7-ea2faeb0539b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bdbe1a-3cf7-4431-9576-e2bb7d5cd5d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5577499-b9af-43a3-94fb-e266107863fe",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to revisit the Milestone 3 Reflection section in their project planning guide and add on to what they wrote the previous class period.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "When students revisit their reflections today, point out that adding new insights after another round of testing is exactly how professional engineers document progress. Encourage them to notice what changed, what improved, and what new questions emerged—this shows they’re thinking like real developers who learn from every test cycle to make smarter, more informed decisions.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5577499-b9af-43a3-94fb-e266107863fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bdbe1a-3cf7-4431-9576-e2bb7d5cd5d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90a3bb46-236c-4ee9-88c0-e7621543873a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s one thing you still need to check or polish before your app feels ready to share?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students self-assess their progress and identify final next steps, using available resources and the rubric to guide productive final work time.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90a3bb46-236c-4ee9-88c0-e7621543873a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6277ec54-655c-4104-abd9-d6ea032e566a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 3 of the project. Explain that today is focused on Milestone 4 – Finalize, where students polish their app and make their code ready for showcasing. Remind students to check their Reactive Logic Project Guide for the Milestone 4 deliverables.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to the last two pages of their Reactive Logic Project Guide, where the project rubric is located. Have students review each category carefully and mark where they believe their project currently falls. Encourage honesty — the goal is to identify strengths and areas for continued growth as intentional designers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are engaging thoughtfully with each rubric criterion, not rushing to completion.\n",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that self-assessment isn't just about scoring their project — it's a professional habit that helps them take ownership of their design choices. Say something like: \"Designers constantly evaluate their own work to make sure it aligns with their goals, audience, and purpose. This rubric helps you see where you've grown and what you can still refine.\"\nIf time allows, have students highlight one category they feel especially proud of and one they'd like to improve next time.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6277ec54-655c-4104-abd9-d6ea032e566a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "095c6347-e5a2-4832-9b12-73af88bcfb6d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "095c6347-e5a2-4832-9b12-73af88bcfb6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2cfdf282-f8ef-4767-b19e-0b0c1c87daf3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 4 to finish developing their Reactive Logic projects. Explain that this is their final dedicated work session for Milestone 4 – Finalize. Reinforce that today's goal is to prioritize improvements based on their self-assessment and TAG peer feedback, rather than trying to fix everything at once. Instruct students to use the feedback they received from their TAG review and self-assessment to finalize their Reactive Logic project. Have students submit their projects at the end of the allotted time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task. Remind students to comment their code with meaningful descriptions. Have students review their rubric and prioritize work on any rows where they do not yet show extensive evidence.\n",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 4",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that professional designers always prioritize fixes before a release. Say something like: \"You don't need to fix everything today — focus on what will have the biggest impact for your user. Iteration is about making smart choices with the time you have.\"\nIf time allows at the end, invite students to do a brief pair preview, where they open a peer's site and give one final piece of positive or constructive feedback.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2cfdf282-f8ef-4767-b19e-0b0c1c87daf3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97c6ef0a-eb3f-4b3f-a874-3ae27ddf0e82",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97c6ef0a-eb3f-4b3f-a874-3ae27ddf0e82",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc062f16-d3b0-4bf7-baff-ae8d531d0bda",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Distribute several sticky notes to each student for the gallery walk.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students begin the Gallery Walk by displaying their Reactive Logic Apps on their computers, then moving around the room to explore classmates' projects. As they visit each one, students should use their sticky notes to leave feedback, kudos, or questions following the TAG approach (Tell, Ask, Give). Encourage them to engage with a range of projects—not just those of close friends.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move around the room to observe how students are engaging with the displayed projects. Encourage students to leave a note about what they find interesting or insightful about their peers' work. Ensure that students are thoughtfully engaging with a variety of projects, not just those of their closest peers.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame the Gallery Walk as a celebration of learning and creativity, not a critique. Say something like: \"This is your chance to see how everyone's ideas came to life in different ways. You might even get inspired by someone else's approach!\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc062f16-d3b0-4bf7-baff-ae8d531d0bda",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a866bef0-661a-4672-9a0d-9b18ab31cf91",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a866bef0-661a-4672-9a0d-9b18ab31cf91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fb787925-c33b-4c45-871b-47f9900af207",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You've gone from planning and designing to building, testing, and refining. This reflection is your chance to capture how you've grown — not just as a coder or designer, but as someone who collaborates with AI intentionally.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fb787925-c33b-4c45-871b-47f9900af207",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b8ee2d5c-5b77-476e-85b0-4ee7153d545e",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Final Reflection and Final Self-Check sections in their Reactive Logic Project Guide. Explain that this final step helps them consolidate their learning and ensure their site meets key design goals — usability, consistency, accessibility, and thoughtful use of AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to elaborate on their answers with specific examples of how they used feedback or iteration to improve their project. If students finish early, prompt them to help peers finalize their submissions or review their rubric one more time for completeness.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "You can use the Reactive Logic Planning Guide as evidence for the Algorithmic Planning rubric criteria. The Final Reflection can be used as evidence for the Process and Reflection rubric criteria.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b8ee2d5c-5b77-476e-85b0-4ee7153d545e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e3ecb35-db6d-4a65-ae41-e03690cd3598",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Unit Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
